import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"the kernel calculus as a package, and the layer ladder","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"the kernel calculus as a package, and the layer ladder","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z","status":"proposed","status_note":"proposed — **revised** (see *Revision*, which reorders the"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/adrs/0009-kernel-calculus-and-layers.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/adrs/0009-kernel-calculus-and-layers.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/adrs/0009-kernel-calculus-and-layers.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0009-—-the-kernel-calculus-as-a-package-and-the-layer-ladder" tabindex="-1">ADR 0009 — the kernel calculus as a package, and the layer ladder <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0009-—-the-kernel-calculus-as-a-package-and-the-layer-ladder" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR 0009 — the kernel calculus as a package, and the layer ladder”">​</a></h1><p>stages and fixes the naming rule). <strong>Gated on ADR 0010</strong>: the interpretation model (&quot;one language, many interpretations&quot;) is a prerequisite — there is no bespoke VM, and the <code>Machine</code> port below is superseded by 0010&#39;s <code>Calculus</code> + per-interpretation entry points. <strong>Depends on</strong>: ADR 0002 (categorical re-expression), ADR 0003 (four dimensions — <em>and</em> its lesson that a port with no consumer is decoration), ADR 0004 (abstraction as move), ADR 0005 (the open game), ADR 0007 (semantic-atom minting), ADR 0008 (combinator presentation), <strong>ADR 0010 (one language, many interpretations — the prerequisite)</strong>. <strong>Related</strong>: <code>catopt_core.ports</code> (27 protocols), the <code>lab/bridge</code> combinator prototype, plan 0025 (the open frontier), the import-linter layer contract in <code>pyproject.toml</code>.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>Measured, this week:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>package</th><th>lines</th><th>modules</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>catopt-discovery</strong></td><td><strong>50,490</strong></td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td>catopt-core</td><td>28,789</td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td>catopt-orchestrator</td><td>14,953</td><td>17</td></tr><tr><td>catopt-carriers</td><td>14,454</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>catopt-torch</td><td>10,670</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>catopt-onnx</td><td>4,882</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>catopt-native</td><td>403</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>catopt-cuda</td><td>107</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two structural facts follow.</p><ol><li><p><strong><code>catopt-discovery</code> is the largest package in the repository</strong> and it holds four unrelated concerns: the discovery pipeline (<code>census</code>/<code>propose</code>/<code>oracle</code>/<code>impact</code>/<code>emit</code>/<code>evidence</code>/<code>coherence</code>/ <code>vocab</code>), the <strong>minting</strong> stack (<code>antiunify</code>/<code>handler_synth</code>/ <code>grammar</code>/<code>dspec_synth</code>/<code>object_synthesis</code>/<code>cellstore</code>/<code>handl</code>), the <strong>game/RL</strong> layer (<code>engine</code>/<code>players</code>/<code>play</code>/<code>arena</code>/<code>meta_arena</code>/ <code>open_game</code>/<code>training</code>), and <strong>Triton codegen</strong> (<code>genkernel</code>). The hexagonal boundary is enforced <em>between</em> packages; inside this one there is no boundary at all.</p></li><li><p><strong>The ports cover execution and evaluation, and nothing else.</strong> The 27 protocols in <code>catopt_core.ports</code> describe <code>Source</code>/<code>Sink</code>/ <code>Binding</code>/<code>Executor</code>/<code>Composer</code>/<code>Meter</code>/<code>Runner</code>/<code>Criterion</code>/ <code>Profiler</code>/<code>Policy</code>/<code>PerformanceModel</code>/<code>Verifier</code>. There is <strong>no port for the calculus, the search engine, the proposer, or the RL environment</strong> — the four things the project&#39;s own thesis is about.</p></li></ol><p>Meanwhile the thing that most deserves the name <em>kernel</em> exists only as a prototype in <code>lab/bridge/combinator.py</code>: a monoidal basis with explicit <code>dup</code> (Δ) and a bounded <code>loop</code> (trace), with <code>dag ↔ comb</code> bridges. ADR 0008 measured that it round-trips 321/321 corpus workloads and that the RetNet carrier spells as a <code>loop</code> term — but nothing in the package graph reflects that this is the project&#39;s core object language.</p><h3 id="why-lindley–wadler–yallop-is-the-right-basis" tabindex="-1">Why Lindley–Wadler–Yallop is the right basis <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-lindley–wadler–yallop-is-the-right-basis" aria-label="Permalink to “Why Lindley–Wadler–Yallop is the right basis”">​</a></h3><p>Hughes&#39; arrows (&quot;Generalising monads to arrows&quot;, 2000) are the abstraction; <strong>Lindley, Wadler &amp; Yallop&#39;s <em>The Arrow Calculus</em></strong> (2008; and <em>Idioms are Oblivious, Arrows are Meticulous, Monads are Promiscuous</em>, ENTCS 2011) is the part we actually need, because it supplies a <strong>term calculus with an equational theory</strong> rather than a typeclass: commands, a typing discipline, and laws. A kernel language needs exactly that — a syntax to rewrite <em>over</em> and equations to rewrite <em>with</em>. Hughes&#39; own framing is also the honest one for this project: arrows are the <em>meticulous</em> notion — they make sharing and sequencing explicit — which is what <code>dup</code>/<code>loop</code> in our basis are for.</p><h3 id="what-the-language-is-for-—-a-prerequisite-not-an-engine" tabindex="-1">What the language is for — a prerequisite, not an engine <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-language-is-for-—-a-prerequisite-not-an-engine" aria-label="Permalink to “What the language is for — a prerequisite, not an engine”">​</a></h3><p>State this plainly, because it bounds the claim. A semantic categorical language buys exactly three things:</p><ol><li><strong>Uniformity</strong> — laws become <em>equations over morphisms</em> (derivable from structure: monoid → associativity, comonoid → duplication, trace → fold), so the space is generatable rather than hand-listed; search becomes enumeration over one space.</li><li><strong>First-class sharing and iteration</strong> — <code>dup</code> (Δ) and <code>loop</code> (trace) make recurrence forms <em>expressible</em> instead of hand-minted. This is what makes <strong>algorithmic</strong> (fold/scan) forms reachable, as opposed to algebraic identities.</li><li><strong>Soundness</strong> — a discovery is an equation carrying a replayable certificate, so &quot;found&quot; and &quot;correct&quot; stay separable measurements.</li></ol><p>Measured, that yields: <strong>law-level discovery</strong> (a corpus-minted law, guard synthesized, adversarially swept, shipping in <code>DEFAULT</code>; 11 machine-admitted laws; the axiom/lemma split derived) and <strong>equational derivation</strong> (derivations are data, <code>verify_certificate</code> replays them, <code>lemma_cert</code> replays lemmas, the sweep falsifies). It does <strong>not</strong> yield: a smaller search space (a universal language enumerates <em>more</em> — proposers, exposure screening and RL remain the bottleneck), new <em>semantics</em> without a constructor, general (non-equational) proof synthesis, or <strong>algorithm-level novelty</strong> — the largest result to date (the RetNet carrier) is a <strong>rediscovery</strong> of a published duality, and the measured yield of fresh paying structure is ~1.5% of the exposed corpus.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Adopt a two-axis package organization, give the calculus/search/proposal/environment axes ports in core, and promote the calculus to a package only when its own gate says it earns one.</strong></p><h3 id="the-naming-rule-the-two-axes" tabindex="-1">The naming rule (the two axes) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-naming-rule-the-two-axes" aria-label="Permalink to “The naming rule (the two axes)”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>axis</th><th>rule</th><th>packages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>dimension</strong></td><td>dimension-named</td><td><code>catopt-core</code> (primitives + ports) · <code>catopt-&lt;calculus&gt;</code> (the kernel/laws) · <code>catopt-search</code> · <code>catopt-eval</code> · <code>catopt-orchestrator</code> · <code>catopt-discovery</code> · <code>catopt-game</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>domain</strong></td><td><code>catopt-&lt;domain&gt;</code></td><td><strong><code>catopt-torch</code> · <code>catopt-onnx</code> · <code>catopt-cuda</code></strong> — each <em>is</em> the execution dimension for its domain</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>exec-</code> is the <strong>role</strong>, the domain name is the <strong>package</strong>; the domain name wins. <code>catopt-native</code> keeps its name: it is a <em>search</em> implementation (<code>Engine</code>), not a domain.</p><p><strong>Where a thing goes:</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>if it is…</th><th>it lives in…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a law / equivalence / rewrite</td><td>the <strong>dimension</strong> package (semantic side)</td></tr><tr><td>how to <em>run</em> an op for a domain</td><td>the <strong>domain</strong> package</td></tr><tr><td>an engine for the search dimension</td><td>a <strong>dimension</strong> implementation (<code>catopt-native</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>a backend that mints code</td><td>a <strong>domain</strong> package (Triton is torch-adjacent)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="ports-to-add-in-core-see-revision-2-—-most-already-exist" tabindex="-1">Ports to add in core (see Revision 2 — most already exist) <a class="header-anchor" href="#ports-to-add-in-core-see-revision-2-—-most-already-exist" aria-label="Permalink to “Ports to add in core (see Revision 2 — most already exist)”">​</a></h3><p>Each lands <strong>with a conformance test that the current implementation already satisfies</strong> — ADR 0003&#39;s lesson: an unconsumed port is decoration.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>port</th><th>shape</th><th>consumer today (unported)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Calculus</code></td><td>the one object language: term/rule structure, shapes, transforms</td><td>ADR 0010; <code>Rewrite</code>/<code>derivation</code>/<code>typing</code> today — <strong>interpretations</strong> (eval/types/rewrite/lower/cost) read it; there is no separate <code>Machine</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Engine</code></td><td><code>saturate(cells, budget) -&gt; Graph</code></td><td><code>catopt-native</code> (already <code>engine=</code> opt-in)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Proposer</code></td><td><code>propose(graph, budget) -&gt; iterable[Candidate]</code></td><td><code>enumerate</code>/<code>forms</code>/<code>propose</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Environment</code> + <code>Reward</code></td><td><code>reset/step/observe</code>; <code>reward(state, action)</code></td><td><code>engine.Board</code>, <code>arena</code>, <code>meta_arena</code>, <code>open_game</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Prover</code> / <code>Bound</code></td><td>per-artifact verification boundaries</td><td><code>verify_certificate</code>; bounded members</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="revision-2026-10-14-—-what-the-first-draft-got-wrong" tabindex="-1">Revision (2026-10-14) — what the first draft got wrong <a class="header-anchor" href="#revision-2026-10-14-—-what-the-first-draft-got-wrong" aria-label="Permalink to “Revision (2026-10-14) — what the first draft got wrong”">​</a></h3><p>Writing the first draft made two weaknesses visible, and one of them reorders the whole plan.</p><ol><li><strong>The deeper inversion is <code>catopt-carriers</code>, not <code>catopt-discovery</code>.</strong> 14k lines hold carrier <strong>laws</strong> (semantics) <em>and</em> executors (execution) inside a <strong>torch-coupled</strong> package — i.e. semantics depend on an execution library, which is the opposite of the thesis. The equational part belongs on the semantic side; the executors belong in the domain package. <strong>This is the first split</strong>, because it is the one that makes &quot;semantic categorical language&quot; true rather than aspirational.</li><li><strong>A peer kernel package hides a decision instead of making it.</strong> Two representations (op-graph IR + arrow calculus) mean every law must exist in both, or one is second-class — exactly the drift ADR 0008 warned about. The architectural question is <strong>which representation is canonical</strong>, and that is its own decision record, not a package move. Until it is answered, the kernel stays a lab prototype.</li><li><strong>A package for one module is over-fragmentation.</strong> A <code>catopt-kernels</code> holding only <code>genkernel</code> means a pyproject, a floor, an import contract and a CI surface for one module. Fold it into a domain package instead.</li><li><strong>Organizational resilience is weaker than verification resilience.</strong> Minted laws now sit in <code>DEFAULT</code>: a change to the bank silently changes extraction for every caller (AGENTS.md has to warn about it), and there is no versioning or compatibility story for the bank or for minted atoms. No package split addresses this; it is recorded here as the standing gap.</li></ol><h3 id="revision-2-2026-10-14-—-most-of-the-ports-to-add-already-exist" tabindex="-1">Revision 2 (2026-10-14) — most of the &quot;ports to add&quot; already exist <a class="header-anchor" href="#revision-2-2026-10-14-—-most-of-the-ports-to-add-already-exist" aria-label="Permalink to “Revision 2 (2026-10-14) — most of the &quot;ports to add&quot; already exist”">​</a></h3><p>Checking the list against the code before writing any of it (ADR 0003&#39;s lesson applied to this ADR&#39;s own proposal):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>proposed port</th><th>measured state</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Engine</code></td><td><strong>already exists</strong> — <code>catopt_core.ports.Engine</code> (plan 0010 lever 3): <code>add_term</code> / <code>find</code> / <code>run</code> / <code>extract_best</code>, with the pure-Python <code>EGraph</code> as the reference and <code>catopt-native</code> as the opt-in</td></tr><tr><td><code>Environment</code> + <code>Reward</code></td><td><strong>already exist</strong> — <code>catopt_discovery.engine.Board</code> (<code>observe</code> / <code>step -&gt; (state, report)</code>) plus the <code>Report</code> dataclass carrying <code>reward</code> + <code>terminal</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Calculus</code></td><td>its <strong>surface is already named</strong> — <code>RuleLike</code> / <code>RuleSetLike</code> (laws as data) and <code>ShapeRule</code> (shape inference). What is missing is not a protocol but a <strong><code>Language</code> <em>value</em></strong> — a data structure (ADR 0010), not a port</td></tr><tr><td><code>Prover</code> / <code>Bound</code></td><td><code>Verifier</code> <strong>exists</strong> (the semantic gate). <code>Bound</code> (the certified-approximation boundary) is genuinely absent — and its consumer (the bounded-member path) lives in the orchestrator, so it is named there or not at all</td></tr><tr><td><code>Proposer</code></td><td>genuinely absent — and it cannot be a real port yet: its shape would be <code>propose(...) -&gt; Iterable[Candidate]</code>, but <code>Candidate</code> lives in <code>catopt_discovery.proposal</code>, so a core-owned <em>view</em> (the way <code>VerifyResult</code> is the core-owned view of a verify report) must exist first</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>So one of the six already existed in core, two existed elsewhere, one is a data structure rather than a port, and one needs a core-owned shape before it can be honest.</strong> The ports are therefore <strong>tied to the stages that create their consumers</strong>, not added up front:</p><ul><li><code>Proposer</code> — when the proposal layer&#39;s candidate shape is promoted (a core-owned <code>Candidate</code> view), i.e. with stage 1&#39;s <em>data</em> work, not before.</li><li><code>Environment</code> / <code>Reward</code> — when the game layer is extracted into <code>catopt-game</code> (stage 5): a package cannot depend on a protocol it does not own, and the port belongs where the drivers live.</li><li><code>Bound</code> — with the bounded-member path, if it needs naming at all.</li><li><code>Calculus</code> — not a port: a <code>Language</code> value (ADR 0010). Its tables&#39; read surfaces are the ports that already exist.</li></ul><h3 id="staged-plan-revised-order" tabindex="-1">Staged plan (revised order) <a class="header-anchor" href="#staged-plan-revised-order" aria-label="Permalink to “Staged plan (revised order)”">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>Ports + conformance tests</strong> — cheap, no behaviour change, no import churn.</li><li><strong>Fix the carrier inversion</strong> — carrier <em>laws</em> to the semantic side, carrier <em>executors</em> into the domain package.</li><li><strong>Adopt the interpretation model</strong> (ADR 0010) — one language, many engines; and <strong>decide the canonical representation</strong> (its own ADR) — op-graph IR vs arrow calculus. <em>Then</em>, and only then:</li><li><strong>Promote the calculus</strong> to a dimension-named package — <strong>gated on ADR 0008 gate 3</strong> (does search <em>reach</em> <code>loop</code>-headed forms?). If the gate measures negative, the calculus is a presentation win and this stage is not justified. <strong>MEASURED 2026-10-16: the gate is negative.</strong> A <code>loop</code>-proposing rule over the presentation was built (<code>lab/bridge/loop_propose.py</code>, catopt <code>b7ba9ff</code>); it works on the presentation&#39;s spelled scan, but the search&#39;s winner has no regular spelled-scan subterm and the presentation&#39;s spelled form is not even in the search&#39;s root e-class — so the rule fires 0× and no <code>loop</code>-headed winner is reached. <strong>Stage 4 is therefore not justified by the presentation&#39;s discovery value</strong>; it may proceed only on a different argument, and a <em>form-level</em> proposer (which abstracts the step, rather than a syntactic e-graph rewrite) is the piece of work that would re-open the gate. See <code>project/retros/gate3-loop-proposing-rule.md</code>. <strong>Re-measured 2026-10-16 (later): that form-level proposer was built (<code>lab/bridge/index_abstract.py</code>, catopt <code>e4a193a</code>) — a parametric abstraction binding the index <em>and</em> the recurrence state as parameters. It collapses a <em>regular</em> fold to one shared step and the closure reaches a <code>Loop</code> (control PASS), but the RetNet winner still does not collapse: its 7 blocks span two chunk sizes and extraction mixed two state spellings, so no constant-parameter abstraction can unite them. Stage 4 is still not justified, and the missing piece is larger than this bullet implied. See <code>project/retros/gate3-parametric-abstraction.md</code>. <strong>Re-measured 2026-10-16 (later still): still not justified.</strong> The block width was abstracted (<code>514888c</code>) and the winner is still 7 distinct templates <em>even modulo the width and the index</em> — the residual is the state spelling (the cross term is the unfolded geometric sum), not a parameter. See <code>project/retros/gate3-sizes-abstraction.md</code>.</strong></li><li><strong>Extract <code>catopt-game</code></strong> — the RL layer out of the 50k-line package (the clear navigability win).</li><li><strong>Fold <code>genkernel</code> into a domain package</strong> — no <code>catopt-kernels</code>.</li><li>Each stage updates the import-linter layer contract; <strong>each new package gets its own coverage ratchet</strong> (the discovery-style <code>--fail-under</code> climb), never a global widening.</li></ol><h2 id="falsification" tabindex="-1">Falsification <a class="header-anchor" href="#falsification" aria-label="Permalink to “Falsification”">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Gate 3 decides the calculus.</strong> If search cannot reach <code>loop</code>-headed forms from a corpus graph, stage 4 is not justified. Measure before spending the coverage budget. <strong>Measured 2026-10-16: negative</strong> — the proposing rule fires 0× on the RetNet export (the winner has no regular spelled-scan subterm; the presentation&#39;s spelled form is in a different e-class). Stage 4 is not justified by the discovery value of the presentation. See ADR 0008&#39;s gate-3 FAIL bullet and <code>project/retros/gate3-loop-proposing-rule.md</code>. <strong>Re-measured 2026-10-16 (later): the form-level proposer was built and the gate is <em>still</em> negative</strong> — the parametric abstraction collapses a regular fold but not the RetNet winner (two chunk sizes, two state spellings). See ADR 0008&#39;s second gate-3 FAIL bullet and <code>project/retros/gate3-parametric-abstraction.md</code>. <strong>Re-measured again 2026-10-16 (later still): still negative, and one candidate fix is retired.</strong> The &quot;two state spellings&quot; cause was measured to be a cost <em>tie</em> broken by op name, and a principled sharing-aware extraction recovers <strong>1.01 %</strong> of the winner&#39;s FLOPs (455,752 → <strong>451,128</strong>; <s>5.06 % / 432,696</s> was a hash-order artifact, corrected 2026-10-15 by <code>abf6114</code>; numerically equivalent to 2.2e-16) while leaving the gate FAIL. The mix is a cascade of per-class ties, and the small-chunk family&#39;s unfactored spelling is a genuine cost optimum — so the spelling cannot be normalised by a tie-break. Stage 4 is still not justified by the presentation&#39;s discovery value. See ADR 0008&#39;s third gate-3 FAIL bullet and <code>project/retros/gate3-extraction-state-spelling.md</code>. <strong>Re-measured once more 2026-10-16: still negative, and the &quot;two chunk widths&quot; cause is retired.</strong> The block width (<code>sizes</code>) was abstracted in the move (ADR 0008&#39;s fourth gate-3 FAIL bullet, catopt <code>514888c</code>) and the winner&#39;s 7 blocks are still <strong>7 distinct templates</strong> even modulo the width <em>and</em> the index — the difference is structural (the cross term is the unfolded geometric sum), not a parameter. Stage 4 is still not justified, and the remaining piece is the state spelling alone. See <code>project/retros/gate3-sizes-abstraction.md</code>. <strong>Re-measured 2026-10-16 (fifth move, <code>9ad7c3f</code>): the gate PASSES, with one named residual.</strong> The form-level proposer (<code>lab/bridge/form_propose.py</code>) uses the saturated e-graph as the anti-unification oracle — the factored state spellings extraction never picked were already derived members. It anti-unifies the 7-block family into one step schema (<code>pack(S′, o)</code>, 9 wires), emits <code>unroll(step, 3)</code> per chain, and <code>comb_to_dag(prog)</code> lands in the winner&#39;s own e-class 25 (<code>whole_equal: True</code>). Residual: <code>state-chain-broken:block3→block4</code> — the small-family terminal state and the large-family initial state are the same value but saturation never merged them (a reach gap, not a fold defect), so the result is <strong>two <code>Loop(step, 3)</code> under one schema, not one <code>Loop(step, 6)</code></strong>. Stage 4&#39;s falsification blocker no longer fires outright — the calculus <em>is</em> a reachable search space, with the honest bound that extraction still never <em>selects</em> a <code>loop</code> member (the fold is proposed and e-class-certified, not cost-chosen). See <code>project/retros/gate3-form-level-proposer.md</code>.</li><li><strong>A port with no consumer is decoration.</strong> If <code>Calculus</code>/<code>Machine</code>/ <code>Engine</code>/<code>Proposer</code>/<code>Environment</code> cannot name an existing consumer and a conformance test, they do not land.</li><li><strong>The split must pay in <em>change surface</em>, not file counts.</strong> For a representative change (add a law; add a backend; retrain a prior), count the packages it touches before and after. If that number does not fall, the split was cosmetic. This is the <strong>acceptance test</strong>, not a footnote.</li><li><strong>No layer inversion.</strong> The import-linter contract stays acyclic; a calculus package that imports a domain package is a failed calculus.</li></ol><h2 id="honest-scars" tabindex="-1">Honest scars <a class="header-anchor" href="#honest-scars" aria-label="Permalink to “Honest scars”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Splitting 14k–50k lines is a test project, not a <code>git mv</code> project.</strong> The coverage floors make each extraction cost tests; budget accordingly.</li><li><strong>The calculus&#39;s discovery value is unproven</strong> — this ADR proposes a home for it, not evidence that it earns one.</li><li><strong>The language is a prerequisite, not an engine</strong> (above): it makes discoveries expressible, composable and checkable; it does not shrink the search space, invent semantics, or prove general theorems.</li><li><strong>The bank has no versioning story</strong> — minted laws in <code>DEFAULT</code> are load-bearing for every caller.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The thesis becomes structural.</strong> &quot;Programs are 1-cells, laws are 2-cells, derivations are 3-cells&quot; stops being prose and becomes a package with a port, an interpreter and a rewrite machine.</li><li><strong>Discovery gains a uniform enumeration space</strong> — morphism shapes rather than op-patterns (ADR 0008&#39;s intent), which is what gate 3 is waiting on.</li><li><strong>Execution adapters multiply cleanly.</strong> The domain axis stays <code>catopt-&lt;domain&gt;</code>; a new backend implements <code>Sink</code>, and a code-minting backend is just another domain package.</li><li><strong>Learning becomes optional, not embedded.</strong> The RL layer can be dropped or replaced without touching the pipeline it steers.</li></ul>`,40)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};